.TH "VIRTUALBRICKS\-CONTROL" "7" "2026\-10\-07" "virtualbricks 3.0.0.dev3" "Virtualbricks Manual"
.SH NAME
virtualbricks\-control \- the protocols of the control sockets: JSON,
AMP 1 and AMP 2
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]virtualbricks\f[R] \f[B]\-\-listen\f[R]
\f[B]unix:\f[R]\f[I]path\f[R]\f[B]:protocol=json\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]virtualbricks\f[R] \f[B]\-\-listen\f[R]
[\f[B]unix:\f[R]\f[I]path\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]virtualbricks\f[R] \f[B]\-\-listen\f[R]
\f[B]tcp:\f[R]\f[I]port\f[R][\f[B]:protocol=json\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A Virtualbricks started with \f[B]\-\-listen\f[R] answers on control
sockets: the commands of its console, from \f[B]virtualbricks
\-\-command\f[R] or from any program, and what the windows of another
Virtualbricks ask.
A socket carries lines of JSON or the boxes of AMP, Twisted\(aqs
Asynchronous Messaging Protocol, and on them three protocols:
.TP
\f[B]JSON\f[R]
Lines of JSON, on a socket of \f[B]protocol=json\f[R]: a line of the
console in each request, the lines it prints in each answer.
Any language that reads and writes JSON speaks it, and a shell with
\f[B]socat\f[R](1).
.TP
\f[B]AMP 1\f[R]
On an AMP socket, as a socket is without \f[B]protocol=json\f[R]:
\f[B]Hello\f[R], who answers, and \f[B]Run\f[R], a line of the console
and its lines.
\f[B]virtualbricks \-\-command\f[R] speaks it, and so can a program
written with Twisted.
.TP
\f[B]AMP 2\f[R]
On the same connection, once \f[B]Hello\f[R] agrees on it: a typed
command for each command of the console, as \f[B]BrickStart\f[R] with
its names, and the commands of the windows of \f[B]virtualbricks
\-\-connect\f[R]: \f[B]Follow\f[R], which sends the project, then each
change, and the others.
.IP
.EX
  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
  |     JSON     |  |    AMP 1    |           AMP 2           |
  |  line, cwd   |  | Hello, Run  | typed commands, Follow    |
  |  \-> lines    |  |             | and its pushes, Attach    |
  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
  |  the token,  |  |  the token, if asked: Challenge and     |
  |  if asked    |  |  Authenticate                           |
  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
  |  JSON lines  |  |  AMP boxes                              |
  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
  |  a unix, tcp or ssl socket of \-\-listen                    |
  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
     protocol=json      protocol=amp, the default
.EE
.PP
A \f[B]tcp\f[R] socket answers only a client that proves first that it
knows the token of Virtualbricks, in either format.
This page describes the protocols for whoever writes a client: the
sockets (\f[B]THE SOCKETS\f[R]), the proof of the token (\f[B]THE
TOKEN\f[R]), the JSON protocol (\f[B]THE JSON PROTOCOL\f[R]), the boxes
of AMP (\f[B]AMP\f[R]) and the commands of its two protocols (\f[B]AMP
PROTOCOL 1\f[R], \f[B]AMP PROTOCOL 2\f[R]), how they change
(\f[B]VERSIONS\f[R]) and how much they carry (\f[B]LIMITS\f[R]).
How to open the sockets, and which, is in \f[B]THE CONTROL SOCKET\f[R]
of \f[B]virtualbricks\f[R](1).
.SH THE SOCKETS
The protocols are the same on each type of socket: \f[B]unix\f[R], a
path that only its user can connect to; \f[B]tcp\f[R], a port of this
machine; and \f[B]ssl\f[R], a port inside TLS.
Each \f[B]tcp\f[R] socket asks a client for the token, and so may an
\f[B]ssl\f[R] socket, see \f[B]virtualbricks\f[R](1); a \f[B]unix\f[R]
socket never does.
The first message tells the client which, see \f[B]THE TOKEN\f[R].
The socket of \f[B]\-\-listen\f[R] alone,
\f[I]$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR\f[R]/virtualbricks/\f[I]key\f[R]/.control, speaks
AMP.
.PP
Each connection runs its requests one after the other, in the order they
came: a client may send the next request before the answer to the one
before, and the answers come in the same order.
Over AMP, the commands that only read the machine answer at once, beside
those that wait, see \f[B]AMP PROTOCOL 2\f[R].
Connections don\(aqt wait for each other, nor for the terminal or the
events of Virtualbricks.
.PP
The paths of a command, as that of \f[B]source\f[R] \f[I]file\f[R], are
read from \f[B]cwd\f[R], an absolute path that a request may give, or
else from the folder where Virtualbricks runs.
A client on another machine leaves \f[B]cwd\f[R] out: its folders
aren\(aqt those of Virtualbricks.
Virtualbricks answers in its own language, and logs each command it
gets, with the address of the client on a network socket.
.PP
When Virtualbricks quits, it closes each connection once the answers it
owes are written; a client that doesn\(aqt read them within 2 seconds is
cut off.
\f[B]quit\f[R] answers too, and a connection that follows Virtualbricks
gets \f[B]Quitting\f[R] first.
.SH THE TOKEN
The token is a line of text of at least 16 characters, in a file:
\f[I]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R]/virtualbricks/token, or the file of
\f[B]tokenFile=\f[R] in the description of the socket.
The first start of a socket that needs it makes the file, with 43 random
characters.
The file must be a regular file of the user that nobody else can read or
change, mode 0600; the spaces around the line don\(aqt count.
A client reads the same token: copy the file to its machine.
.PP
Neither end sends the token.
Each proves that it knows it, over two nonces, a random number of each
end in lowercase hex digits: Virtualbricks sends one of 64 digits, and
the client\(aqs has from 32 to 128.
The proof of a \f[I]side\f[R], \f[B]client\f[R] or \f[B]server\f[R], is
the HMAC\-SHA256, under the token in UTF\-8, of the text
.IP
.EX
virtualbricks side S C
.EE
.PP
where \f[I]S\f[R] is the nonce of Virtualbricks and \f[I]C\f[R] that of
the client, one space between the words, written in lowercase hex
digits:
.IP
.EX
  client                               Virtualbricks
    |                                        |
    |<\-\- nonce S \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|
    |                                        |
    |    nonce C, HMAC(token,                |
    |    \(dqvirtualbricks client S C\(dq)         |
    |\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|  checks it: if
    |                                        |  wrong, closes
    |                                        |  the connection
    |<\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|
    |    HMAC(token,                         |
    |    \(dqvirtualbricks server S C\(dq)         |
  checks it: if wrong,
  closes the connection
.EE
.PP
The client proves first, and Virtualbricks then proves it to the client:
a program that took the port in place of Virtualbricks doesn\(aqt know
the token, so it can\(aqt, and the client doesn\(aqt send it any
command.
With new nonces each time, a proof is good for one connection only, and
nobody who reads it learns the token.
A client has 10 seconds from connecting to give a right proof; then the
connection is closed.
.PP
In a shell, \f[B]openssl\f[R](1) computes the proof of the client:
.IP
.EX
printf \(aqvirtualbricks client %s %s\(aq \(dq$nonce\(dq \(dq$mine\(dq |
    openssl dgst \-sha256 \-hmac \(dq$token\(dq
.EE
.SH THE JSON PROTOCOL
.SS Lines
Each message is a JSON object on a line of its own, in UTF\-8, ended by
a newline, LF.
Virtualbricks skips the empty lines it gets, and closes the connection
on a line longer than 64 KiB, 65536 bytes.
A key that a message doesn\(aqt need is ignored: a client ignores the
keys it doesn\(aqt know too, so that a later version can add some.
.SS The greeting
Virtualbricks writes the first line as soon as a client connects:
.IP
.EX
{\(dqprotocol\(dq: 1, \(dqversion\(dq: \(dq3.0.0\(dq, \(dqpid\(dq: 4200,
 \(dqproject\(dq: \(dqlab1\(dq}
.EE
.TP
\f[B]protocol\f[R]
1, the version of the JSON protocol.
A client that doesn\(aqt know it sends nothing, and closes the
connection.
.TP
\f[B]version\f[R]
The version of Virtualbricks.
.TP
\f[B]pid\f[R]
Its process.
.TP
\f[B]project\f[R]
The name of the open project, or \f[B]null\f[R].
.SS Requests and answers
A request has the command, \f[B]line\f[R], a line of the console as it
is typed, and \f[B]cwd\f[R], optional, the folder of its paths.
Its answer has \f[B]ok\f[R], whether the command was done,
\f[B]lines\f[R], what it printed, a text for each line, without
newlines, and \f[B]error\f[R], why it failed, when \f[B]ok\f[R] is
\f[B]false\f[R]; \f[B]lines\f[R] is then what it printed before it
failed:
.IP
.EX
{\(dqline\(dq: \(dqbrick start sw1\(dq, \(dqcwd\(dq: \(dq/home/alice/labs\(dq}
{\(dqok\(dq: true, \(dqlines\(dq: [\(dqsw1 runs, process 4242\(dq]}
{\(dqline\(dq: \(dqbrick start vm9\(dq}
{\(dqok\(dq: false, \(dqlines\(dq: [], \(dqerror\(dq: \(dqNo brick named vm9\(dq}
.EE
.PP
A line that isn\(aqt a request gets an answer with \f[B]ok\f[R]
\f[B]false\f[R] and why, as \(dqNot a request: \(dqcwd\(dq is not an
absolute path\(dq, and the connection goes on.
.SS With the token
On a socket that asks for the token, the first line asks for the proof,
with the nonce of Virtualbricks, and the client answers with its own
nonce and its proof.
The greeting follows a right proof, with the proof of Virtualbricks:
.IP
.EX
{\(dqprotocol\(dq: 1, \(dqauth\(dq: \(dqtoken\(dq, \(dqnonce\(dq: \(dq3f9a...\(dq}
{\(dqnonce\(dq: \(dqc41d...\(dq, \(dqproof\(dq: \(dq8e02...\(dq}
{\(dqprotocol\(dq: 1, \(dqversion\(dq: \(dq3.0.0\(dq, \(dqpid\(dq: 4200,
 \(dqproject\(dq: \(dqlab1\(dq, \(dqproof\(dq: \(dq51b7...\(dq}
.EE
.PP
A wrong proof, a line that isn\(aqt one, or none in 10 seconds gets an
answer with \f[B]ok\f[R] \f[B]false\f[R] and why, as \(dqWrong
token\(dq, and the connection closes.
.IP
.EX
  client                               Virtualbricks
    |                                        |
    |<\-\- {\(dqprotocol\(dq: 1, \(dqauth\(dq: \(dqtoken\(dq, \-\-\-|  if it asks
    |    \(dqnonce\(dq: S}                         |  for the token
    |\-\-\- {\(dqnonce\(dq: C, \(dqproof\(dq: ...} \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|
    |                                        |
    |<\-\- {\(dqprotocol\(dq: 1, \(dqversion\(dq: ..., \-\-\-\-|  the greeting
    |    \(dqpid\(dq: ..., \(dqproject\(dq: ...          |
    |    [, \(dqproof\(dq: ...]}                   |
    |                                        |
    |\-\-\- {\(dqline\(dq: \(dqbrick start sw1\(dq} \-\-\-\-\-\-\->|
    |\-\-\- {\(dqline\(dq: \(dqstatus\(dq} \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|
    |<\-\- {\(dqok\(dq: true, \(dqlines\(dq: [...]} \-\-\-\-\-\-\-|  in the same
    |<\-\- {\(dqok\(dq: true, \(dqlines\(dq: [...]} \-\-\-\-\-\-\-|  order
.EE
.SH AMP
.SS Boxes
AMP carries boxes: a key and its value, then another, and so on, up to a
key of no bytes.
Each key and each value has its length first, in two bytes, the high
byte first: a key has from 1 to 255 bytes, a value from 0 to 65535.
A request of \f[B]Hello\f[R], and its answer:
.IP
.EX
   length  key         length  value
  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
  | 00 04 | _ask      | 00 01 | 1                     |
  | 00 08 | _command  | 00 05 | Hello                 |
  | 00 09 | protocols | 00 06 | 00 01 \(dq1\(dq 00 01 \(dq2\(dq   |
  | 00 00 |              the end of the box           |
  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
  | 00 07 | _answer   | 00 01 | 1                     |
  | 00 08 | protocol  | 00 01 | 2                     |
  | 00 07 | version   | 00 05 | 3.0.0                 |
  | 00 03 | pid       | 00 04 | 4200                  |
  | 00 07 | project   | 00 04 | lab1                  |
  | 00 00 |              the end of the box           |
  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
.EE
.PP
A request has \f[B]_ask\f[R], a tag that the program chooses, different
from those of its requests that wait, and \f[B]_command\f[R], the name
of the command; then its arguments, in any order.
Its answer has \f[B]_answer\f[R], the same tag, and the values of the
answer; or, if the command failed, \f[B]_error\f[R], the tag,
\f[B]_error_code\f[R] and \f[B]_error_description\f[R], why.
The tags pair the answers with the requests, which may be answered in
another order.
A request without \f[B]_ask\f[R] wants no answer: the pushes of
\f[B]Follow\f[R] are such.
A program written with Twisted has all this in
\f[B]twisted.protocols.amp\f[R]; in Python without Twisted,
\f[B]virtualbricks.console.ampbox\f[R] reads and writes the boxes.
.SS Values
A value is bytes, read as the command says:
.TP
\f[B]str\f[R]
A text, in UTF\-8.
.TP
\f[B]int\f[R]
An integer, in decimal digits, with \f[B]\-\f[R] before it if negative.
.TP
\f[B]bool\f[R]
\f[B]True\f[R] or \f[B]False\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]bytes\f[R]
Bytes, as they are.
.TP
\f[B][str]\f[R], \f[B][int]\f[R]
A list: each item with its length in two bytes, one after the other.
.TP
\f[B]pairs\f[R]
A list of records, each with \f[B]key\f[R] and \f[B]value\f[R], both
\f[B]str\f[R]: each record a box, with its end, one after the other.
.PP
An optional argument, written \f[B]?\f[R] after its kind, is left out of
the box when it has no value, and so is an optional value of an answer.
.SS Errors
\f[B]_error_code\f[R] is one of the codes of the command, which name
what went wrong; each protocol below lists its own.
Twisted adds two: \f[B]UNHANDLED\f[R], there is no command of that name,
as a command of a later version or of a protocol that the connection
doesn\(aqt speak, see \f[B]VERSIONS\f[R]; and \f[B]UNKNOWN\f[R], a
failure that Virtualbricks didn\(aqt expect, after which the connection
closes.
A box that isn\(aqt one closes the connection too.
.SH AMP PROTOCOL 1
The commands of protocol 1 are in the module
\f[B]virtualbricks.console.ampwire\f[R], which loads nothing but
Twisted\(aqs \f[B]amp\f[R]: a program imports it, or copies it, as
\f[B]THE CONTROL SOCKET\f[R] of \f[B]virtualbricks\f[R](1) does.
They are written here as in \f[I]tests/data/amp\-protocol\-2.txt\f[R] of
the sources: each argument as \f[I]name\f[R]:\f[I]kind\f[R], then
\f[B]\->\f[R] and what the answer has:
.IP
.EX
Hello         protocols:[int]?
              \-> protocol:int version:str pid:int project:str?
Run           line:str cwd:str? \-> lines:[str]
Challenge     \-> nonce:str
Authenticate  nonce:str proof:str \-> proof:str
.EE
.TP
\f[B]Hello\f[R]
Who answers, and the protocol of the connection from then on.
\f[B]protocols\f[R] are those the program speaks; \f[B]protocol\f[R] is
the highest that Virtualbricks speaks too, 1 without them or when it
speaks none of them.
Then the \f[B]version\f[R] of Virtualbricks, its \f[B]pid\f[R], and the
open \f[B]project\f[R], left out if none is.
A connection that doesn\(aqt call \f[B]Hello\f[R] speaks protocol 1.
.TP
\f[B]Run\f[R]
A line of the console and its \f[B]cwd\f[R], as in a request of the JSON
protocol; the answer is the lines it printed.
If the command fails, the error says why, and what it printed before is
lost.
.TP
\f[B]Challenge\f[R]
The nonce of Virtualbricks, for the proof of the token.
.TP
\f[B]Authenticate\f[R]
The nonce of the program and its proof; the answer is the proof of
Virtualbricks, which the program checks, see \f[B]THE TOKEN\f[R].
.PP
Their errors:
.TP
\f[B]COMMAND_FAILED\f[R]
\f[B]Run\f[R]: the command failed, or its \f[B]cwd\f[R] isn\(aqt an
absolute path.
.TP
\f[B]ANSWER_TOO_LONG\f[R]
\f[B]Run\f[R]: the command was done, but its lines are longer than the
65535 bytes of a value; a JSON socket carries them.
.TP
\f[B]TOKEN_NEEDED\f[R]
\f[B]Hello\f[R], \f[B]Run\f[R] and the commands of protocol 2: on a
socket that asks for the token, each fails until the program proves that
it knows it.
.TP
\f[B]WRONG_TOKEN\f[R]
\f[B]Challenge\f[R] and \f[B]Authenticate\f[R]: the socket takes no
token, and the connection goes on; or the proof is wrong, or
\f[B]Authenticate\f[R] came without a \f[B]Challenge\f[R] before it, and
the connection closes.
.IP
.EX
  program                              Virtualbricks
    |                                        |
    |\-\-\- Challenge \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|  if it asks
    |<\-\- nonce S \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|  for the token
    |\-\-\- Authenticate nonce C, proof \-\-\-\-\-\-\->|
    |<\-\- proof of Virtualbricks \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|
    |                                        |
    |\-\-\- Hello protocols [1, 2] \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|
    |<\-\- protocol 2, version, pid, ... \-\-\-\-\-\-|
    |                                        |
    |\-\-\- Run line \(dqbrick start sw1\(dq \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|  protocol 1
    |<\-\- lines [...] \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|
    |\-\-\- BrickStart name [\(dqvm1\(dq] \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|  protocol 2
    |<\-\- lines [...] \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|
.EE
.SH AMP PROTOCOL 2
Once \f[B]Hello\f[R] has agreed on 2, a connection speaks the commands
of protocol 1 and those of protocol 2; before, these fail with
\f[B]PROTOCOL_NEEDED\f[R].
They are in two modules that load nothing else of Virtualbricks but
\f[B]ampwire\f[R]: \f[B]virtualbricks.console.ampcommands\f[R], the
typed commands, and \f[B]virtualbricks.remote.commands\f[R], those of
the windows; a program imports them, or copies them.
Each command of protocol 2 has the same errors:
.TP
\f[B]NOT_FOUND\f[R]
A name of the command names nothing there: a brick, an event, an image,
a project, a program or a file.
Nothing was done.
.TP
\f[B]BAD_ARGUMENT\f[R]
An argument isn\(aqt one the command takes; or the box has a key that
the command doesn\(aqt have, or lacks one it needs, which Twisted would
drop, or close the connection on.
Nothing was done.
.TP
\f[B]PROTOCOL_NEEDED\f[R]
\f[B]Hello\f[R] hasn\(aqt agreed on protocol 2.
.TP
\f[B]COMMAND_FAILED\f[R]
The command failed on the way: a program or a file said no.
Part of it may be done.
.TP
\f[B]ANSWER_TOO_LONG\f[R]
The command was done, but its answer is longer than the 65535 bytes of a
value.
.TP
\f[B]TOKEN_NEEDED\f[R]
As in protocol 1.
.PP
In the modules, \f[B]NotFound\f[R] and \f[B]BadArgument\f[R] are
subclasses of \f[B]CommandFailed\f[R]: a program that catches
\f[B]CommandFailed\f[R] catches the three.
.SS The typed commands
A typed command for each command of the console.
Its name is the words of the command, each with a capital letter:
\f[B]brick card add\f[R] is \f[B]BrickCardAdd\f[R], \f[B]status\f[R] is
\f[B]Status\f[R].
Its arguments are named after those of the command in \f[B]COMMANDS\f[R]
of \f[B]virtualbricks\f[R](1), in lower case, with \f[B]_\f[R] for what
isn\(aqt a letter or a digit: \f[I]NAME\f[R] is \f[B]name\f[R],
\f[I]KEY\f[R]=\f[I]VALUE\f[R] is \f[B]key_value\f[R], \f[B]\-\-at\f[R]
is \f[B]at\f[R].
A number is an \f[B]int\f[R], an argument that repeats a list,
\f[I]KEY\f[R]=\f[I]VALUE\f[R]...
a list of \f[B]pairs\f[R], an option without a value a \f[B]bool\f[R],
the rest a \f[B]str\f[R]; what is optional in the console is optional
here.
Each takes \f[B]cwd\f[R] too, as \f[B]Run\f[R] does, and answers
\f[B]lines:[str]\f[R], the lines of the console:
.IP
.EX
BrickTypes
BrickList
BrickNew           kind:str name:str?
BrickShow          name:str
BrickKeys          kind_name:str key:str?
BrickSet           name:str key_value:pairs
BrickUnset         name:str key:[str]
BrickStart         name:[str]
BrickStop          name:[str]
BrickKill          name:[str]
BrickRestart       name:[str]
BrickPause         name:[str]
BrickContinue      name:[str]
BrickSuspend       vm:str
BrickResume        vm:str
BrickReset         vm:str
BrickMonitor       name:str
BrickConnect       name:str target:[str]
BrickDisconnect    name:str end:str?
BrickCardAdd       vm:str kind:str options:[str]?
BrickCardSet       vm:str card:str key_value:pairs
BrickCardRemove    vm:str card:[str]
BrickRename        name:str new:str
BrickDuplicate     name:str new:str?
BrickDelete        name:[str]
EventList
EventNew           name:str?
EventShow          name:str
EventSet           name:str key_value:pairs
EventActionAdd     name:str what:str subject:str? at:int?
EventActionRemove  name:str n:[int]
EventActionMove    name:str n:int to:int
EventStart         name:[str]
EventStop          name:[str]
EventRun           name:[str]
EventRename        name:str new:str
EventDuplicate     name:str new:str?
EventDelete        name:[str]
ProjectList
ProjectShow
ProjectOpen        name:str
ProjectNew         name:str
ProjectSave
ProjectRename      name:str new:str
ProjectDuplicate   name:str new:str
ProjectDelete      name:str force:bool?
Help               topic:[str]?
Status
Quit
Source             file:str
ImageList
ImageAdd           name:str path:str key_value:pairs?
ImageShow          name:str
ImageSet           name:str key_value:pairs
ImageRename        name:str new:str
ImageDelete        name:str
SettingShow        key:str?
SettingSet         key_value:pairs
SettingUnset       key:[str]
.EE
.PP
What each does and prints is what \f[B]virtualbricks\f[R](1) says of its
command.
\f[B]BrickSet\f[R](name=\(dqvm1\(dq, key_value=[{\(dqkey\(dq:
\(dqmemory\(dq, \(dqvalue\(dq: \(dq1024\(dq}]) is \f[B]brick set vm1
memory=1024\f[R]; \f[B]BrickStart\f[R](name=[\(dqsw1\(dq, \(dqvm1\(dq])
is \f[B]brick start sw1 vm1\f[R].
.SS The commands of the windows
What the windows of \f[B]virtualbricks \-\-connect\f[R] need that the
console has no command for.
A table, a state and the other JSON of this section are JSON in a
\f[B]str\f[R], since AMP has no tables:
.IP
.EX
Follow             \-> project:str? version:str
Apply              kind:str name:str changes:str links:str
                   extras:str \->
Connect            source:str target:str \-> connected:bool
QemuFacts          program:str \-> path:str version:str
                   options:str machines:str cpus:str devices:str
                   displays:str audio_drivers:str netdevs:str
                   accelerators:str
MachineProperties  program:str machine:str \-> text:str
UsbDevices         \-> devices:str
ImageFacts         path:str \-> file:str info:str others:str
MakeImage          path:str format:str size:int \->
StartOver          vm:str device:str \-> trashed:bool
TrashFile          path:str \-> trashed:bool
Relink             name:str path:str \->
ProjectNames       \-> names:[str]
ProjectSummary     name:str \-> summary:str
DiskUsage          name:str \-> private_disks:int other_files:int
Readme             \-> text:str
SetReadme          text:str \->
ReadmePicture      name:str path:str offset:int
                   \-> data:bytes size:int
SetKsm             enable:bool \-> enabled:bool
Folder             path:str \-> entries:[str] more:bool
ProgramsFound      vde_path:str qemu_path:str
                   \-> vde:str qemu:str
Attach             brick:str console:str \->
.EE
.TP
\f[B]Follow\f[R]
Send the project, then each change, see \f[B]Follow and the pushes\f[R];
the answer is the open project, and the version of Virtualbricks.
.TP
\f[B]Apply\f[R]
The OK of the panel of a brick, an event or an image, \f[I]kind\f[R]
\f[B]brick\f[R], \f[B]event\f[R] or \f[B]image\f[R]: what the panel
changed, all or nothing.
\f[B]changes\f[R] is an object of the settings changed, by their names
and with their values as the project file writes them, see
\f[B]virtualbricks\-config\f[R](5); \f[B]links\f[R], a list of the plugs
moved, each [\f[I]index\f[R], \f[I]target\f[R]], the target written as
in the project file, \f[B]\(dq\(dq\f[R] for none; \f[B]extras\f[R], what
a brick has beyond its settings, only when it changed: the
\f[B]cards\f[R] of a machine, each {\f[B]kind\f[R], \f[B]model\f[R],
\f[B]mac\f[R], \f[B]connect\f[R], \f[B]link\f[R]}, and the
\f[B]states\f[R], \f[B]transitions\f[R] and \f[B]period\f[R] of a
Netemu.
Only the keys given change: the last OK wins for them, and the rest
keeps what another window set.
.TP
\f[B]Connect\f[R]
Connect brick \f[I]source\f[R] to brick \f[I]target\f[R], as a drop
does: a free plug, or a new card of a machine.
Whether it could.
.TP
\f[B]QemuFacts\f[R]
What the QEMU program \f[I]program\f[R], as
\f[B]qemu\-system\-x86_64\f[R], prints: its \f[B]path\f[R], and for each
question a JSON list [\f[I]out\f[R], \f[I]err\f[R], \f[I]status\f[R]], a
value each, since all of them are more than a value carries.
.TP
\f[B]MachineProperties\f[R]
What \f[B]\-machine\f[R] \f[I]type\f[R]\f[B],help\f[R] prints, for
\f[I]machine\f[R], or for the default type if it is \f[B]\(dq\(dq\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]UsbDevices\f[R]
The USB devices of the machine: a JSON list of {\f[B]id\f[R],
\f[B]description\f[R]}.
.TP
\f[B]ImageFacts\f[R]
What the file \f[I]path\f[R] is: \f[B]file\f[R], {\f[B]size\f[R],
\f[B]mtime\f[R], \f[B]taken\f[R]}, the time in nanoseconds and the space
it takes in bytes; \f[B]info\f[R], what \f[B]qemu\-img info
\-\-output=json\f[R] prints; \f[B]others\f[R], a JSON list of
[\f[I]project\f[R], \f[I]image\f[R]], the images of the other projects
of the workspace with that file.
.TP
\f[B]MakeImage\f[R]
Make the file of an empty disk, of \f[I]format\f[R] \f[B]qcow2\f[R] or
\f[B]raw\f[R], and of \f[I]size\f[R] bytes.
\f[B]BAD_ARGUMENT\f[R] if the file is there already.
.TP
\f[B]StartOver\f[R]
The private copy of disk \f[I]device\f[R] of machine \f[I]vm\f[R] goes
to the trash, or is deleted without one: whether it went to the trash.
.TP
\f[B]TrashFile\f[R]
A file of the workspace that no image uses goes to the trash, or is
deleted without one: whether it went to the trash.
.TP
\f[B]Relink\f[R]
Give image \f[I]name\f[R] the file \f[I]path\f[R], and the private
copies made on the image with it.
.TP
\f[B]ProjectNames\f[R]
The names of the projects of the workspace, sorted.
.TP
\f[B]ProjectSummary\f[R]
What the list of the projects shows of one, as a JSON object:
\f[B]name\f[R], \f[B]path\f[R], \f[B]description\f[R],
\f[B]modified\f[R], \f[B]bricks\f[R] (a count for each type),
\f[B]events\f[R] (a count), \f[B]images\f[R] (each {\f[B]name\f[R],
\f[B]path\f[R], \f[B]found\f[R]}) and \f[B]problem\f[R], \f[B]null\f[R]
unless the project file can\(aqt be read.
.TP
\f[B]DiskUsage\f[R]
The space that a project takes, in bytes: its private disks, and the
other files.
.TP
\f[B]Readme\f[R], \f[B]SetReadme\f[R]
The README of the open project; the new one is written when the project
is saved.
.TP
\f[B]ReadmePicture\f[R]
A picture of the README of project \f[I]name\f[R], \f[I]path\f[R]
relative to its folder, see \f[B]virtualbricks\-config\f[R](5): its
\f[B]data\f[R] from byte \f[I]offset\f[R], as much as a value carries,
and the \f[B]size\f[R] of the file.
A program asks again from the end of what came, until it has
\f[B]size\f[R] bytes.
\f[B]BAD_ARGUMENT\f[R] for a path that leads out of the folder, a file
that isn\(aqt one or that is larger than 10 MB.
.TP
\f[B]SetKsm\f[R]
Turn KSM on or off; whether it runs afterwards.
.TP
\f[B]Folder\f[R]
What completes \f[I]path\f[R] as it is typed: the entries of its folder
that start with its last part, a folder with a \f[B]/\f[R] after it, at
most 200, and whether there are more.
.TP
\f[B]ProgramsFound\f[R]
What the folders \f[I]vde_path\f[R] and \f[I]qemu_path\f[R] hold, before
they are the settings, as the Settings window shows them: for each,
\f[B]vde\f[R] and \f[B]qemu\f[R], an object \f[B]{folder, exists, found,
missing}\f[R], whether the folder is there, the programs found, by name,
with their path, in the folder or else in \f[B]PATH\f[R], and those
missing, each a pair of the program and its package, \f[B]null\f[R] if
no package has it.
.TP
\f[B]Attach\f[R]
Join the program to a console of a running brick, see \f[B]Attach\f[R].
.PP
\f[B]QemuFacts\f[R], \f[B]MachineProperties\f[R], \f[B]UsbDevices\f[R],
\f[B]ImageFacts\f[R], \f[B]DiskUsage\f[R], \f[B]ReadmePicture\f[R],
\f[B]Folder\f[R] and \f[B]ProgramsFound\f[R] change nothing: they answer
as soon as they can, beside the requests that wait.
The others wait for their turn, as the typed commands do, and
Virtualbricks logs them.
.SS Follow and the pushes
\f[B]Follow\f[R] asks Virtualbricks for its project and its changes.
It sends them as \f[I]pushes\f[R]: commands that it calls on the
program, without \f[B]_ask\f[R], that want no answer.
A program that follows declares the eight of them, and has a responder
for each: Twisted closes the connection on a command it has no responder
for.
.IP
.EX
Opened             project:str? settings:str machine:str
Changed            kind:str name:str table:str state:str
Renamed            kind:str old:str new:str
Removed            kind:str name:str
Synced
SettingsChanged    settings:str
Logged             message:str
Quitting
.EE
.TP
\f[B]Opened\f[R]
A project is open, and the copy starts over: its name, left out if none
is; \f[B]settings\f[R], a JSON object of the settings of Virtualbricks
and of the project, by their names in
\f[B]virtualbricks\-config\f[R](5); and \f[B]machine\f[R], a JSON object
of what the machine of Virtualbricks has: \f[B]version\f[R],
\f[B]workspace\f[R], \f[B]project_folder\f[R], \f[B]trash\f[R] (whether
what is removed goes to a trash), \f[B]runtime_dir\f[R],
\f[B]workspace_runtime_dir\f[R], \f[B]missing\f[R] (the programs it
lacks), \f[B]qemu_programs\f[R] (the names of its QEMU programs),
\f[B]lacks\f[R] (for each type of brick, \f[B]null\f[R] or the programs
it lacks, {\f[B]line\f[R], \f[B]text\f[R]}) and \f[B]ksm\f[R] (whether
KSM runs).
.TP
\f[B]Changed\f[R]
An image, a brick or an event, \f[I]kind\f[R] \f[B]image\f[R],
\f[B]brick\f[R] or \f[B]event\f[R], is new or changed: its
\f[B]table\f[R], as the project file writes it, and its \f[B]state\f[R],
what the project file doesn\(aqt write: {\f[B]file\f[R]}, the
\f[B]size\f[R], \f[B]mtime\f[R] and \f[B]taken\f[R] of the file of an
image, \f[B]null\f[R] if it isn\(aqt there; {\f[B]pid\f[R],
\f[B]copies\f[R]}, the process of a brick, \f[B]null\f[R] if it
doesn\(aqt run, and the private copies of a machine, as \f[B]file\f[R],
by device; {\f[B]left\f[R]}, the seconds that an event still waits,
\f[B]null\f[R] if it doesn\(aqt.
.TP
\f[B]Renamed\f[R]
An image, a brick or an event has a new name.
.TP
\f[B]Removed\f[R]
A brick or an event is deleted, an image removed.
.TP
\f[B]Synced\f[R]
The whole project has been sent.
.TP
\f[B]SettingsChanged\f[R]
The settings changed: all of them, as in \f[B]Opened\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]Logged\f[R]
A message of the log, info and above, as a JSON object: \f[B]time\f[R],
in seconds since 1970, \f[B]level\f[R], \f[B]text\f[R],
\f[B]traceback\f[R] or \f[B]null\f[R], \f[B]namespace\f[R],
\f[B]source\f[R] and \f[B]source_type\f[R], the brick it is about or
\f[B]null\f[R], \f[B]pid\f[R], and \f[B]stream\f[R], \f[B]stdout\f[R] or
\f[B]stderr\f[R] for what the process of a brick printed.
.TP
\f[B]Quitting\f[R]
Virtualbricks quits.
.PP
\f[B]Follow\f[R] sends \f[B]Opened\f[R], a \f[B]Changed\f[R] for each
image, brick and event, then \f[B]Synced\f[R]: the images first, then
the bricks, each after those it plugs into, then the events.
Then the last 500 messages of the log, and the answer.
After that, Virtualbricks gathers what changes and sends it once a turn
of its loop, each object once, as it is then: the renames and the
deletes first, in the order they came, then what changed, in the order
above, the settings, the messages of the log, and \f[B]Quitting\f[R].
Before it answers a command of the connection, it sends what waits: once
a command is answered, the program has what it changed.
When another project opens, the copy starts over, with \f[B]Opened\f[R];
\f[B]Follow\f[R] again does the same.
A push longer than a value carries isn\(aqt sent, and the log says so.
.IP
.EX
  program                              Virtualbricks
    |                                        |
    |\-\-\- Follow \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|
    |<\-\- Opened project \(dqlab1\(dq \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|  the project
    |<\-\- Changed image debian \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|
    |<\-\- Changed brick sw1 \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|
    |<\-\- Changed brick vm1 \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|
    |<\-\- Synced \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|
    |<\-\- Logged, the last 500 \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|
    |<\-\- project, version \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|  the answer
    |                                        |
    |\-\-\- BrickStart name [\(dqvm1\(dq] \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|
    |<\-\- Changed brick vm1 \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|  what it
    |<\-\- Changed image debian \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|  changed
    |<\-\- lines [...] \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|  the answer
    |                                        |
    |<\-\- Changed brick vm1 \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|  once a turn
    |<\-\- Logged \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|
    |                                        |
    |<\-\- Quitting \-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-|  at the end
.EE
.SS Attach
\f[B]Attach\f[R] joins the program to a console of a running brick:
\f[B]monitor\f[R], its control monitor, or \f[B]serial\f[R], the serial
socket of a machine.
Virtualbricks connects to the console, then answers; from then on, the
connection carries the bytes of the console both ways, and nothing else:
AMP\(aqs \f[B]ProtocolSwitchCommand\f[R].
When either end closes, so does the other.
So a program opens a new connection for each console, proves the token
if asked, agrees on protocol 2 with \f[B]Hello\f[R], then calls
\f[B]Attach\f[R].
\f[B]NOT_FOUND\f[R] says there is no such brick, \f[B]BAD_ARGUMENT\f[R]
that it doesn\(aqt run or has no such console, \f[B]COMMAND_FAILED\f[R]
that Virtualbricks can\(aqt reach the console.
The windows of \f[B]virtualbricks \-\-connect\f[R] join the connection
to a terminal of their own, as \f[B]vdeterm\f[R](1):
.IP
.EX
  terminal     program            Virtualbricks     console
     |            |                     |              |
     |            |\-\- Attach brick vm1, |              |
     |            |   console monitor \->|\-\- connects \->|
     |            |<\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\- answer \-|              |
     |<= bytes ==>|<====== bytes ======>|<== bytes ===>|
.EE
.SH VERSIONS
The JSON protocol is 1, in the greeting; another would have another
number.
Over AMP, \f[B]Hello\f[R] agrees on the protocol: the highest that both
ends speak.
1 is always one of them.
.PP
Protocol 2 keeps its commands as they are.
A later Virtualbricks may add commands to it, and optional arguments to
its commands, and nothing else: an older one answers a command it
doesn\(aqt have with \f[B]UNHANDLED\f[R], and an argument it doesn\(aqt
know with \f[B]BAD_ARGUMENT\f[R], so a program that sends them knows
that they weren\(aqt done.
A change of any other kind makes protocol 3, which would take the place
of 2: a program that speaks both calls \f[B]Hello\f[R] with
\f[B]protocols\f[R] [1, 2, 3].
The sources keep the commands of protocol 2 in
\f[I]tests/data/amp\-protocol\-2.txt\f[R], which the tests compare with
the modules.
.PP
The windows of \f[B]virtualbricks \-\-connect\f[R] also want the same
version of Virtualbricks at both ends: the \f[B]version\f[R] in the
answer of \f[B]Hello\f[R].
.SH LIMITS
.TP
A line of the JSON protocol
64 KiB, 65536 bytes; a longer one closes the connection.
.TP
A key of AMP
255 bytes.
.TP
A value of AMP
65535 bytes.
A longer answer is \f[B]ANSWER_TOO_LONG\f[R], after the command was
done; a longer push isn\(aqt sent.
.TP
The proof of the token
10 seconds from connecting.
.TP
The last answers, at the end
2 seconds for the client to read them.
.TP
The log, for a program that follows
The last 500 messages, then each new one.
.SH EXAMPLES
A shell on a JSON socket, with \f[B]socat\f[R](1); the lines it prints
are the greeting and the answers:
.IP
.EX
virtualbricks \-\-no\-gui \-\-listen \(rs
    unix:\(ti/labs/lab1.json:protocol=json &
socat \- UNIX\-CONNECT:$HOME/labs/lab1.json
{\(dqprotocol\(dq: 1, \(dqversion\(dq: \(dq3.0.0\(dq, \(dqpid\(dq: 4200, ...}
{\(dqline\(dq: \(dqbrick new switch sw1\(dq}
{\(dqok\(dq: true, \(dqlines\(dq: [\(dqsw1\(dq]}
{\(dqline\(dq: \(dqbrick start sw1\(dq}
{\(dqok\(dq: true, \(dqlines\(dq: [\(dqsw1 runs, process 4242\(dq]}
.EE
.PP
A client of the JSON protocol in Python, without anything else, for a
Virtualbricks started with \f[B]\-\-listen tcp:8765:protocol=json\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
import hmac, json, os, secrets, socket, sys


def proof(token, side, nonce, mine):
    text = f\(dqvirtualbricks {side} {nonce} {mine}\(dq
    key = token.encode()
    return hmac.new(key, text.encode(), \(dqsha256\(dq).hexdigest()


def send(lines, **message):
    lines.write(json.dumps(message).encode() + b\(dq\(rsn\(dq)
    lines.flush()


def receive(lines):
    return json.loads(lines.readline())


def connect(port, token):
    sock = socket.create_connection((\(dq127.0.0.1\(dq, port))
    lines = sock.makefile(\(dqrwb\(dq)
    first = receive(lines)
    if first.get(\(dqauth\(dq) == \(dqtoken\(dq:
        nonce, mine = first[\(dqnonce\(dq], secrets.token_hex(32)
        send(lines, nonce=mine,
             proof=proof(token, \(dqclient\(dq, nonce, mine))
        first = receive(lines)
        expected = proof(token, \(dqserver\(dq, nonce, mine)
        given = first.get(\(dqproof\(dq, \(dq\(dq)
        if not hmac.compare_digest(given, expected):
            sys.exit(first.get(\(dqerror\(dq, \(dqWrong token\(dq))
    if first.get(\(dqprotocol\(dq) != 1:
        sys.exit(\(dqIt doesn\(aqt speak the JSON protocol 1\(dq)
    return lines


def ask(lines, line):
    send(lines, line=line)
    answer = receive(lines)
    print(\(dq\(rsn\(dq.join(answer[\(dqlines\(dq]))
    if not answer[\(dqok\(dq]:
        sys.exit(answer[\(dqerror\(dq])


path = os.path.expanduser(\(dq\(ti/.config/virtualbricks/token\(dq)
with open(path) as file:
    token = file.read().strip()
lines = connect(8765, token)
ask(lines, \(dqstatus\(dq)
ask(lines, \(dqbrick list\(dq)
.EE
.PP
A program written with Twisted that speaks protocol 2, for a
Virtualbricks started with \f[B]\-\-listen tcp:8765\f[R]: it starts
\f[B]sw1\f[R], and prints what changes until Virtualbricks quits:
.IP
.EX
import json, os

from twisted.internet import defer, endpoints, task
from twisted.protocols import amp

from virtualbricks.console.ampcommands import BrickStart
from virtualbricks.console.ampwire import Hello, authenticate
from virtualbricks.remote import commands


class Follower(amp.AMP):
    \(dq\(dq\(dqPrints what changes; a responder for each push.\(dq\(dq\(dq

    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()
        self.lost = defer.Deferred()

    def connectionLost(self, reason):
        super().connectionLost(reason)
        self.lost.callback(None)

    \(atcommands.Opened.responder
    def opened(self, project, settings, machine):
        print(\(dqProject\(dq, project)
        return {}

    \(atcommands.Changed.responder
    def changed(self, kind, name, table, state):
        print(kind, name, json.loads(state))
        return {}

    \(atcommands.Renamed.responder
    def renamed(self, kind, old, new):
        print(kind, old, \(dqis now\(dq, new)
        return {}

    \(atcommands.Removed.responder
    def removed(self, kind, name):
        print(kind, name, \(dqis gone\(dq)
        return {}

    \(atcommands.Synced.responder
    def synced(self):
        return {}

    \(atcommands.SettingsChanged.responder
    def settings_changed(self, settings):
        return {}

    \(atcommands.Logged.responder
    def logged(self, message):
        print(json.loads(message)[\(dqtext\(dq])
        return {}

    \(atcommands.Quitting.responder
    def quitting(self):
        print(\(dqVirtualbricks quits\(dq)
        return {}


async def main(reactor):
    endpoint = endpoints.TCP4ClientEndpoint(
        reactor, \(dq127.0.0.1\(dq, 8765
    )
    vb = await endpoints.connectProtocol(endpoint, Follower())
    path = os.path.expanduser(\(dq\(ti/.config/virtualbricks/token\(dq)
    with open(path) as file:
        await authenticate(vb, file.read().strip())
    hello = await vb.callRemote(Hello, protocols=[1, 2])
    if hello[\(dqprotocol\(dq] != 2:
        raise SystemExit(\(dqIt doesn\(aqt speak protocol 2\(dq)
    await vb.callRemote(commands.Follow)
    await vb.callRemote(BrickStart, name=[\(dqsw1\(dq])
    await vb.lost


task.react(lambda reactor: defer.ensureDeferred(main(reactor)))
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[I]$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\f[R]/virtualbricks/token
The token of the \f[B]tcp\f[R] and \f[B]ssl\f[R] sockets, see \f[B]THE
TOKEN\f[R].
.TP
\f[I]$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR\f[R]/virtualbricks/\f[I]key\f[R]/.control
The socket of \f[B]\-\-listen\f[R] alone of the workspace of
\f[I]key\f[R], which speaks AMP.
